#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125288} :activity/id #uuid "2f9b26dc-7c78-5bb7-9eb0-e6029679e710" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048449 :topic/id #uuid "a05758d1-f2b4-5a86-b7b0-c2a4bb6dcb85" :topic/math-academy-id 1931} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125288 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125731 :tutorial/content "We can extend our knowledge of limits to multivariable functions. We'll first consider the two-variable case. \n\nWe denote $\\mathbf{x}=(x,y)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_0 = (a,b)$ as a particular, fixed vector. Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined in some neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0.$ \n\nThen, the notation\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\mathbf{x} \\to \\mathbf{x}_0} f (x,y) = L\n$$\n\nmeans that the value of $f$ can be made as close as we want to $L,$ provided that $\\mathbf x$ is sufficiently close to $\\mathbf{x}_0.$\n\nA few things to note:\n\n- This definition of a limit also extends to functions of more than two variables in the natural way.\n\n- All of the rules set out by the algebra of limits are valid for functions of several variables.\n\nWe say that $f(x,y)$ is **continuous** at $(a,b)$ if\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\mathbf{x} \\to (a,b)} f (x,y) = f(a,b).\n$$\n\nSo, if a function is continuous at $(a,b),$ then the limit can be evaluated by simply plugging $(x,y) = (a,b)$ into the function.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's calculate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1)}} \\dfrac{xy}{x^2+y^2}\n$$\n\nThis function is continuous for all $(x,y)$ with the exception of $(0,0)$ where the denominator vanishes. Since the point $(2,1)$ does not cause the denominator to vanish, we can calculate the limit by substituting $(x,y) = (2,1)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "eccfdda1-288a-5169-adde-a00163fa348f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2480 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a0c271d5-ca3c-5651-a78d-a2b18242c45c" :step/math-academy-id 11087 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125289}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125289 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124452 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124256 :question/id #uuid "a5753b43-b288-5cca-a26b-b060917182ab" :question/math-academy-id "e-3330"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f2ac99b-55b2-5742-81ab-d2961db1250f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050987}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124937} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265712} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436129}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Functions at Points of Continuity"} :step/id #uuid "9e271cad-793b-51f0-9124-47a943c42f3e" :step/math-academy-id 11090 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125329}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125290 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124453 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124257 :question/id #uuid "301263ef-3b68-54ac-87aa-5964484f5a14" :question/math-academy-id "e-3331"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5666035-199d-56aa-a6f4-f65a3f847b42" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049927}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125220} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265714} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265715} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377695} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436123} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436128}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Rational Functions by Factoring"} :step/id #uuid "98229d4f-e216-5858-8b87-e9442dce8ede" :step/math-academy-id 11092 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125466}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125291 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125732 :tutorial/content "Recall that for a function of a single variable $f(x),$ if the left and right limits both exist and are equal at some point $x=a$, then this is sufficient to conclude that the limit exists at $x=a.$ \n\nSo, if we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a^-} f(x) = \\lim_{x\\to a^+} f(x) = L,\n$$\n\nthen we may conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a} f(x) = L.\n$$\n\nFor a multivariable function, the situation is not so straightforward. For a function of two variables $f(x,y),$ we may only conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x} \\to \\mathbf{x}_0} f(x,y) = L,\n$$\n\nif we can show that the limit is the same *for all possible paths* on which $\\mathbf x$ can approach $\\mathbf{x}_0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b4b46a0a-5b1c-58eb-aa32-dd0ccd57f7a4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2482 :tutorial/title "The Definition of a Limit: Single Variable Functions vs. Multivariable Functions"} :step/id #uuid "a9b09430-2391-566d-ac41-6360e135665e" :step/math-academy-id 11094 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125467}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125297 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124457 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124261 :question/id #uuid "56539c63-dd81-57c9-b7d2-18c935953203" :question/math-academy-id "e-3366"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29610715-333d-5e0c-9328-eec1d45f56ed" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377708} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436127}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits Along Different Paths for Multivariable Functions"} :step/id #uuid "b6bdeefa-e28b-5e72-8c05-e9f8fb9c7542" :step/math-academy-id 11197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125329 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125754 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, we may need to simplify a limit before we can evaluate it. \n\nFor example, let's consider the limit\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,-1)}} \\dfrac{x^2+2xy}{xy+2y^2}.\n$$\n\nDirect substitution causes this limit to take the indeterminate form $\\dfrac 00$ because the numerator and denominator both evaluate to zero at the given point. \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,-1)}} \\dfrac{x^2+2xy}{xy+2y^2} = \\dfrac{(2)^2 + 2(2)(-1)}{(2)(-1) + 2(-1)^2} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by factoring the numerator and denominator and then canceling a common factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2xy}{xy+2{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,-1)}\\frac{x(x+2y)}{y(x+2y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,-1)}\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,-1)}\\frac{x}{y}=\\frac{2}{-1}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,-1)}} \\dfrac{x^2+2xy}{xy+2y^2} = -2.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8f5c0d1f-e38c-5cd7-97e2-1aaaac5158b4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2883 :tutorial/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Rational Functions by Factoring"} :step/id #uuid "6285e0db-558c-5e11-85ab-0b82dc834963" :step/math-academy-id 15456 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125290}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125466 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124558 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124367 :question/id #uuid "ae167d95-9cfd-5f31-91b1-484becd62677" :question/math-academy-id "e-6239"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d53554f-6d77-56f9-8950-d8e31a7f4fdb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050225}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436124} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436130}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Functions Using Conjugate Multiplication"} :step/id #uuid "af7ce556-917b-54ac-a772-adecff1e36f2" :step/math-academy-id 23425 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125291}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125467 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125809 :tutorial/content "However, it is often easy to show that a limit does not exist for a multivariable function. All we have to do is find two paths on which the limit evaluates to different results. \n\nAs an example, let's consider the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)} \\dfrac{y}{x+y}\n$$\n\nClearly, direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00.$ Let's compute the limit along the paths $y=x$ and $y=x^2.$ We will see that the limit evaluates to different results on these two paths.\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\frac{y}{x+y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{x+x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\frac{y}{x+y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{1+x} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on these two paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{y}{x +y} = \\text{DNE}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8040690e-6d4c-50ac-8b9f-f92615717816" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3960 :tutorial/title "Proving That a Limit Does Not Exist by Evaluating It Along Different Paths"} :step/id #uuid "29991074-71d2-5a37-a971-5f658f37d3ba" :step/math-academy-id 23498 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125297}}] :activity/title "Limits and Continuity of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124724}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407852} :activity/id #uuid "a4f417dd-5f5b-58a7-ab74-5a973fc151bb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048449 :topic/id #uuid "a05758d1-f2b4-5a86-b7b0-c2a4bb6dcb85" :topic/math-academy-id 1931} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407850 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377708 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8958422f-4c02-53af-a3e5-28f8445d5bc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377710} {:answer/id #uuid "6f7dc054-8a42-5213-b120-534bafd7ab86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377711} {:answer/id #uuid "7ad9cc3c-2948-54af-96ab-97ac3dc5dd75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377712} {:answer/id #uuid "de9768d4-de86-55ac-a72b-b0470b48216c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377713} {:answer/id #uuid "1b2466e9-2081-57a2-b9cc-0260610356d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377714}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377713} :answer-field/id #uuid "f14349ec-0b87-5b7c-b87c-2368f3340f77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6323a167-9fc1-5848-81f1-63d34a9c63ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-74965" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=\\frac{{0}^{2}\\cdot 0}{{0}^{3}+{0}^{3}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths. \n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the line $y=2x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=2x}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}+8{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "23171e8d-210d-5c2c-9bfd-bb23fda5150e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407853}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407851 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407848 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1408b7a6-0c53-5b8e-9133-67f17a92be59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407824} {:answer/id #uuid "195220e5-a0bf-53a4-9715-d520b9cc06cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407826} {:answer/id #uuid "4272af7f-33bd-5340-9cab-dc535b5219ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407829} {:answer/id #uuid "6e9919cf-e851-589f-8631-eb084d75d2b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407832} {:answer/id #uuid "af13b1b6-a010-5d4f-9f5a-9200139b2880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407837}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407829} :answer-field/id #uuid "356ed38c-7b44-5965-81ac-2c88dd16e0ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d70f2b2e-153e-52a8-b763-677074637b88" :question/math-academy-id "q-74955" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{xy+2}-2}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{xy+2}-2}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 1+2}-2}{{2}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}-4}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{xy+2}-2}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{xy+2}-2)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)}{({x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{xy+2})^{2}-4}{({x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{xy-2}{(xy-2)(xy+2)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{1}{(xy+2)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{1}{(xy+2)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)} & =\\frac{1}{(2\\cdot 1+2)(\\sqrt{2\\cdot 1+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{16}.$"} :step/id #uuid "61b825c0-1272-5e4c-b3ff-4f0f75ec0886" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407850}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407852 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407847 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "041632fd-d218-5815-9c69-da107f5da160" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407822} {:answer/id #uuid "091e3fce-c81a-50bf-8d66-819204f1ee13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407823} {:answer/id #uuid "4d84526b-47f3-51fb-a168-2cba08c88949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407830} {:answer/id #uuid "e7aeae6b-4c5c-573f-8a2d-c1af8e88ebf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407840} {:answer/id #uuid "f5036257-dfe1-524a-b3ae-379280f91c23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407841}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407840} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b798538-abe6-5e7d-a0f2-143f5a975750" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407842}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a9a907de-0404-5ffb-8362-e9676298070b" :question/math-academy-id "q-36139" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y}\n$$\n\n- The limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{3}$ along the path $y={x}^{2}$\n- The limit evaluates to $-1$ along the path $y=-{x}^{2}$\n- The limit exists and is equal to $\\frac{1}{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y}=\\frac{0}{{0}^{2}+2\\cdot 0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-{x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-{x}^{2}}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2(-{x}^{2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "80843e02-eff6-534a-9c80-13ab1c07a9fb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407851}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407853 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407846 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "194293e4-0f48-56b7-8094-db759002b936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407825} {:answer/id #uuid "638f4f88-2af6-5eb2-9ed5-e5ceca460c65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407831} {:answer/id #uuid "88efc86f-081f-55bf-ae7a-919620a60363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407834} {:answer/id #uuid "ab3f8034-9dc3-50c6-bd5e-b2ea032262ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407835} {:answer/id #uuid "ada75cf0-6667-5096-ac74-83cbe5d47353" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407836}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407834} :answer-field/id #uuid "926f9639-4822-59a3-b1ce-00790077ea5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "113d22b7-0222-5499-8d44-de1af29900a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-129340" :question/problem "Determine ${lim}_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{3}-xy}{{x}^{3}y}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{3}-xy}{{x}^{3}y} & =\\frac{{0}^{3}-2(0)}{{2}^{3}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{3}-xy}{{x}^{3}y} & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{y({y}^{2}-x)}{{x}^{3}y} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{\\cancel{y}({y}^{2}-x)}{{x}^{3}\\cancel{y}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{{0}^{2}-2}{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-\\frac{1}{4}.$"} :step/id #uuid "dd8e3137-e924-50eb-b2e3-83c1445d70ab" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407854}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407854 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407849 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39f90210-da8d-511b-9d38-b3d158c315d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407827} {:answer/id #uuid "4005330f-5c8b-5294-9f96-5e9be2626147" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407828} {:answer/id #uuid "88b04b8f-a6d9-5b86-ba06-a0eb6a004245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407833} {:answer/id #uuid "b72aa7bf-e6dc-52f1-8358-bfb499369454" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407838} {:answer/id #uuid "d17e2afc-964e-5d9b-b663-5d2974b50bec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407839}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407828} :answer-field/id #uuid "e093674e-caf5-5f8d-97ff-ca3172fa6889" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "fac05de1-cec1-5741-a5ec-beb8567ada24" :question/math-academy-id "q-129343" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{y+z}{\\sqrt{x-y}-\\sqrt{x+z}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{y+z}{\\sqrt{x-y}-\\sqrt{x+z}} & =\\frac{1+(-1)}{\\sqrt{2-1}-\\sqrt{2+(-1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{y+z}{\\sqrt{x-y}-\\sqrt{x+z}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{(y+z)(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})}{(\\sqrt{x-y}-\\sqrt{x+z})(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{(y+z)(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})}{(x-y)-(x+z)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{(y+z)(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})}{-(y+z)} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{\\cancel{(y+z)}(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})}{\\cancel{(y+z)}} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z}) & =-(\\sqrt{2-1}+\\sqrt{2+(-1)}) \\\\  & =-(1+1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-2.$"} :step/id #uuid "e8e33d31-3246-51ca-a270-794ee881c78b"}] :activity/title "Limits and Continuity of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407821}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116133} :activity/id #uuid "d6f7c63b-f172-5766-b56a-db8bf2a22464" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048920 :topic/id #uuid "cc887b1d-8841-507d-a99b-b7888c08a675" :topic/math-academy-id 1728} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116133 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116498 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find the inverse of the matrix \n\n![](images/fd/fd9e9dc44e27810ef490a898a7c82ae9296eaf85ac9406cf310afe86638f71e1.svg) \n\nWe can compute the inverse of $A$ using elementary row operations. There are two key advantages to using row operations to compute the inverse of a matrix:\n\n- The method works for any invertible matrix of any size, not just $2\\times 2$ matrices.\n\n- It requires significantly less computation than some alternative methods. This is especially true for large matrices.\n\nHere, we'll focus on the $2\\times 2$ case. We use the following fact:\n\n*The row operations that transform $A$ into the identity matrix $I_2$ also transform $I_2$ into $A^{-1}$*.\n\nSo, to find the inverse of $A,$ we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1:** Write down $\\color{blue}A$ and the identity matrix $I_2$ side-by-side, as shown below:\n![](images/e5/e553bcc265698432452c8d35b79fe9dc70c25010a0bcc6874ad0302ea7033ed7.svg)\n\n**Step 2:** Apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,{I}_{2}\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\,I\\,|\\,{A}^{-1}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A^{-1}$ is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n![](images/b7/b7c08e9a28ebfe456a9d77ccdaf6688893dfd04ba755a989a417c8a43e80ee0c.svg)\n\nWe can easily check our result by computing $AA^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{A}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "fc38ae42-2cd4-53df-9ad5-568d8e3b5fd4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3167 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d8efc5ed-eaaf-514d-b03a-248df406a7d5" :step/math-academy-id 17230 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116136}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116134 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115249 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115078 :question/id #uuid "7c480d6a-93a1-56ec-a75c-56402e872c92" :question/math-academy-id "e-4723"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f42188-bf51-5546-89fb-e33ba47c4b5e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435080}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Row Operations Required To Find the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "e874c19f-8e51-5ff3-b8d2-dd1a0540bbc2" :step/math-academy-id 17231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116135 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115250 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115079 :question/id #uuid "e13311fb-21de-58d4-aed0-18304b48bb0c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4724"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a4b135f-df0d-5443-bb93-796f32c380c6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048742}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329705} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385599} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417379}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix Calculated Using Row Operations"} :step/id #uuid "7cf9141d-1c90-5675-a333-b0aa4e0f3cef" :step/math-academy-id 17232 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116134}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116136 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115251 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115080 :question/id #uuid "ff4b4389-d8ff-5b48-904b-ff405abb4e48" :question/math-academy-id "e-4725"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d78574-a17c-5fc3-8c14-3aaec7753b3c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048252}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115851} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417378}] :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 2x2 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations"} :step/id #uuid "e2dcd832-c069-5309-a7a3-27850adf28e2" :step/math-academy-id 17233 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116135}}] :activity/title "Finding the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix Using Row Operations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115474}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120711} :activity/id #uuid "8313edbb-dabc-5be6-9269-6a1622b71d3d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049436 :topic/id #uuid "f5218ca7-a9d3-5ad9-b17a-ee16b6e2b34a" :topic/math-academy-id 1805} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120711 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121049 :tutorial/content "Suppose we know that a plane $\\Pi$ passes through the point $P(1,1,2)$ and that the vector $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 0,0,3 \\rangle$ is perpendicular to the plane. Let's write down an equation of this plane using the dot product.\n\n![](images/0f/0f8353480099369ad92701d1799f72d46eab4bbe5c17add8cb37a4f34074311b.png)\n\nWe start by considering an arbitrary point $R(x,y,z)$ that lies on the plane. The position vectors of the points $R$ and $P$ are $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle 1,1,2 \\rangle$ respectively. So, the vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =r-⟨1,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis parallel to the plane. Therefore, $\\overrightarrow{PR} \\perp \\mathbf{n},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/28/28b6770bd681257b1030679a421b80e3f3d1e8fbb86d2060b64c7301d0878c5c.png)\n\nNow, recall that if two nonzero vectors are perpendicular, then their dot product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{PR}\\perp \\mathbf{n} \\quad \\Leftrightarrow \\quad \\overrightarrow{PR}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} = 0\n$$\n\nSo, we get the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is called the **vector equation of the plane** that passes through the point $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}.$ In our case, we obtain\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{r} - \\langle 1,1,2 \\rangle) \\cdot \\langle 0,0,3 \\rangle = 0.\n$$\n\n**Note:** Using the properties of the dot product, we can arrive at an alternative vector equation given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot n & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c = \\mathbf{p} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}$ is a (scalar) constant." :tutorial/id #uuid "77d83d72-9202-50d7-af28-72062f00bfa5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3255 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cf165cc4-ae6c-564d-b8e6-051c7aba1faa" :step/math-academy-id 17917 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120712}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120712 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119829 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119640 :question/id #uuid "ba897e22-8104-5772-a0d3-9b062f3592f1" :question/math-academy-id "e-4932"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f5c9c30-1745-5986-b116-e9e70a7c6161" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048954}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262809} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413464} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423177}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of the Plane Perpendicular to a Given Vector"} :step/id #uuid "e689c57c-a11c-582d-bed2-1b5777ad003d" :step/math-academy-id 17918 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120870}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120713 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119830 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119641 :question/id #uuid "a33f6c43-4e25-5d67-9f73-ca99a18fb434" :question/math-academy-id "e-4933"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0d93156-f9be-5e6e-976a-bef30dc892ee" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262837} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338828} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338829} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338830} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338831} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420153}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector That Is Parallel To a Plane"} :step/id #uuid "36217cb6-2731-5a89-84ce-0738c511b417" :step/math-academy-id 17919 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120714}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120714 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119831 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119642 :question/id #uuid "43586a83-879c-5caf-9063-a8f4afbb8b4c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4935"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45cdbb0d-2986-5c05-8d23-dce29363051b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048729}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262858} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338842} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413462}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of the Plane Perpendicular to a Given Straight Line"} :step/id #uuid "1b13bc4d-57be-5d17-841a-4f2645bc3982" :step/math-academy-id 17921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120870 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121120 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a plane with the following normal vector: \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle -1,2,1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nLet's use the scalar product to find a vector that is parallel to this plane.\n\nWe denote our parallel vector as $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle t_1,t_2,t_3 \\rangle.$ Now, since $\\mathbf{v} \\perp \\mathbf{n},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,2,1⟩ & =0 \\\\ -{t}_{1}+2{t}_{2}+{t}_{3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to find a nonzero solution to this equation. To do that, we solve the equation for *one* of the variables (it doesn't matter which one). For example, let's solve our equation for the variable $t_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =2{t}_{2}+{t}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the variable $t_1$ depends on $t_2$ and $t_3,$ which, in turn, can be *any* real numbers. The variables $t_2$ and $t_3$ are called **free variables**.\n\nHence, the general form of our vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=\\langle 2t_2+t_3, \\, t_2, \\, t_3 \\rangle,\n$$\n\nwhere $t_2, t_3 \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nTo find a particular vector parallel to the plane, we can substitute any values for $t_2$ and $t_3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- For example, setting $t_2=0$ and $t_3=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_1= \\langle 1, 0, 1 \\rangle.\n$$\n\n- Or, we could set $t_2=1$ and $t_3=0$ and get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\langle 2, 1, 0 \\rangle.\n$$\n\n**Note:** There are infinitely many vectors parallel to a given plane. In our example, we found only two particular vectors." :tutorial/id #uuid "980ef6f4-542d-5915-84b8-e34f4480ae68" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6411 :tutorial/title "A Vector Parallel To a Plane"} :step/id #uuid "69d22458-d2d5-5c3d-b6fb-4714f2ea1b49" :step/math-academy-id 38733 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120713}}] :activity/title "The Vector Equation of a Plane" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120016}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420157} :activity/id #uuid "77095dee-04b2-5198-bba9-43f589fc9964" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049436 :topic/id #uuid "f5218ca7-a9d3-5ad9-b17a-ee16b6e2b34a" :topic/math-academy-id 1805} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420154 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393043 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "493da2a3-c2b1-5d90-946c-32226dc19e64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨3,-5,-2⟩=⟨-3,4,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393045} {:answer/id #uuid "dc7fa3af-d391-569e-bc17-106f3e9396bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-3,4,-1⟩=⟨3,-5,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393046} {:answer/id #uuid "8fe2b282-7f73-5949-a7f3-cc5ddf1e2530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-3,4,-1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393047} {:answer/id #uuid "949e581b-c362-5470-828e-687788c962cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨3,-5,-2⟩=-27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393048} {:answer/id #uuid "1521f0fb-a511-5bc0-893e-2c6a06cceff9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-3,4,-1⟩=-27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393049}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393049} :answer-field/id #uuid "31ef96c8-9198-5e30-8832-aa55a71dc6d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ca2b2271-1ec0-5a3e-b5ac-687d91668797" :question/math-academy-id "q-70895" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(3,-5,-2)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2-3t \\\\ y=3+4t \\\\ z=4-t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨3,-5,-2⟩.$ Converting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2-3t \\\\ y=3+4t \\\\ z=4-t\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨2,3,4⟩+t\\text{ }{{⟨-3,4,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{v}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe direction vector of the line is $v=⟨-3,4,-1⟩,$ so a normal vector to the plane is \n\n$$\nn=v=⟨-3,4,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-3,4,-1⟩ & =⟨3,-5,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,4,-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-3,4,-1⟩ & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6262b847-bfad-5541-b7d9-0c48d99eb024" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420155}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420155 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262846 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1066db60-12af-5e82-a7c0-d24afa450e35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260024} {:answer/id #uuid "336d23d9-d848-54ca-86e6-782c1b13a369" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260339} {:answer/id #uuid "6bfd83ea-5bb5-57e7-b814-e0e365ce1b44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260820} {:answer/id #uuid "d4093fa7-7b24-5445-bf63-718de7a82e0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261744} {:answer/id #uuid "f03309e1-ca15-5335-aab5-a2535a4337a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261981}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260339} :answer-field/id #uuid "f46674a6-7e2b-5656-b018-7f5bf5e7afa0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262547}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "aa3bed00-1af4-5c46-8c12-264209634c51" :question/math-academy-id "q-127910" :question/problem "A nonzero vector $⟨a,\\,0,\\,c⟩$ is parallel to the plane $r\\cdot ⟨-1,1,3⟩=\\sqrt{7}.$ What is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "We need a vector $v=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $r\\cdot ⟨-1,1,3⟩=\\sqrt{7}$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨-1,1,3⟩.$ So, $v⊥n$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,1,3⟩ & =0 \\\\ -{t}_{1}+{t}_{2}+3{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{1} & ={t}_{2}+3{t}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=⟨{t}_{2}+3{t}_{3},\\,{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{2},{t}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nWe are told that the second component of $v$ equals $0.$ So, we must set ${t}_{2}=0.$ \n\nSince $v$ is nonzero, we can pick any ${t}_{3}\\ne 0.$ For example, setting ${t}_{3}=1$, we get\n\n$$\nv=⟨3,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{a}{c}=3.$"} :step/id #uuid "6f1edd60-137c-5af0-bf08-f3f3e9df0344" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420156}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420156 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420153 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b182a09-cc9d-51c4-95fe-e78790ecf72a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420142} {:answer/id #uuid "36cb63bc-ae79-51e4-b5ab-3039c42340e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420143} {:answer/id #uuid "4173b538-e381-5dd5-b0c5-10dc2eaa96c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420144} {:answer/id #uuid "502b671f-b1be-5d29-8f96-08571a87d173" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420145} {:answer/id #uuid "72001519-6957-56f0-b511-f27a9ed3c85f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420146}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420143} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9a0d8dd-871f-5fc8-9699-7a78c5f80aac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3fa29ff6-5ae4-5642-82fd-03b1645e810c" :question/math-academy-id "q-127921" :question/problem "A nonzero vector $⟨0,\\,b,\\,c⟩$ is parallel to the plane $r\\cdot ⟨5,-1,3⟩=27.$ What is the value of $\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "We need a vector $v=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $r\\cdot ⟨5,-1,3⟩=27$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨5,-1,3⟩.$ So, $v⊥n$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨5,-1,3⟩ & =0 \\\\ 5{t}_{1}-{t}_{2}+3{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{2} & =5{t}_{1}+3{t}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=⟨{t}_{1},\\,5{t}_{1}+3{t}_{3},\\,{t}_{3}⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{1},{t}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nWe are told that the first component of $v$ equals $0.$ So, we must set ${t}_{1}=0.$ \n\nSince $v$ is nonzero, we can pick any ${t}_{3}\\ne 0.$ For example, setting ${t}_{3}=1$, we get\n\n$$\nv=⟨0,\\,3,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{b}{c}=3.$"} :step/id #uuid "7c175861-cf16-5bce-9a3d-7dd7700bfeb3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420158}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420157 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120224 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a8f836e-b1bd-5929-a0d4-cc62e2406547" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨2,-3,5⟩=-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420147} {:answer/id #uuid "8f5b6228-4151-5fb7-a8b8-4defb3112da4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-2,0,3⟩=11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420148} {:answer/id #uuid "ab54b251-064b-57d4-8fc1-3a45c6f498e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨2,-3,5⟩=⟨-2,0,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420149} {:answer/id #uuid "b05ca4a9-4343-56a3-86ae-980793c8602f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-2,0,3⟩=⟨2,-3,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420150} {:answer/id #uuid "f59eba3d-6e2f-504f-905a-a227d63c2c42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨2,-3,5⟩=11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420151}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420148} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2947f04-3e78-507e-b09a-00ccebdf73ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117505}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ee828cf7-8b80-575c-a644-51d699464b23" :question/math-academy-id "q-33037" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $Q(2,-3,5)$ and is perpendicular to the line that goes through the points \n$A(3,2,-8)$ and $B(1,2,-5).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨2,-3,5⟩.$\n\nSince the line  passes through the points $A(3,2,-8)$ and $B(1,2,-5)$, we can find a vector that is parallel to the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨1,2,-5⟩-⟨3,2,-8⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,0,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨-2,0,3⟩.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-2,0,3⟩ & =⟨2,-3,5⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,0,3⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-2,0,3⟩ & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8d1b2442-6684-5f23-a6d5-881beb105644" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420154}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420158 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262859 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65e4dacb-b64f-5663-ae54-844ec9e37c4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨1,-2,4⟩=22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260760} {:answer/id #uuid "a344d6d0-c843-57a0-87ed-6643487a6ce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-2,2,1⟩=12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261298} {:answer/id #uuid "a5b752a4-52ea-54e2-96dc-81dc14098d76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨4,-2,1⟩=18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261313} {:answer/id #uuid "aa9815dd-2b59-5495-91a4-c387e6882786" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨1,-4,-2⟩=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261371} {:answer/id #uuid "c0f71bee-c234-59c3-9e78-d739bf030e68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨2,-2,1⟩=15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261577}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261577} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e498358-75ca-5ef1-ae02-06dee6634086" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7885abf5-baa7-59dd-be20-bec3451c5be4" :question/math-academy-id "q-127956" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $Q(4,-2,3)$ and is  perpendicular to the line \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1+2t \\\\ y=3-2t \\\\ z=5+t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨4,-2,3⟩.$ Converting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1+2t \\\\ y=3-2t \\\\ z=5+t\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨-1,3,5⟩+t\\text{ }{{⟨2,-2,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{v}.\n$$\n\n   \n\nThe direction vector of the line is $v=⟨2,-2,1⟩.$ So, a normal  vector to the plane is \n\n$$\nn=v=⟨2,-2,1⟩.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,-2,1⟩ & =⟨4,-2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨2,-2,1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,-2,1⟩ & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f1fe54dc-0b25-528c-81fb-caa7a89e418f"}] :activity/title "The Vector Equation of a Plane" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420141}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423181} :activity/id #uuid "68a08d63-69d2-59f4-855e-ab9c40ae1bca" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049436 :topic/id #uuid "f5218ca7-a9d3-5ad9-b17a-ee16b6e2b34a" :topic/math-academy-id 1805} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423178 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338831 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ad3af79-fcd3-5749-b0e5-9411379a50d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336721} {:answer/id #uuid "886c099c-bd36-506a-9021-b550fe0b57c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337264} {:answer/id #uuid "926d8af8-a19c-5443-bbf7-6f269886e17b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337364} {:answer/id #uuid "d60f66f3-48fa-5ebf-837a-e090ca7d3d6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337896} {:answer/id #uuid "d99cfee8-de55-5ea6-8c2e-3385468433bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337925}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337896} :answer-field/id #uuid "7727c896-6f1b-5615-b00c-42ad7fd27f9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338483}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e0327785-b28b-5842-b652-0805899123b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-70830" :question/problem "A nonzero vector $⟨a,\\,b,\\,0⟩$ is parallel to the plane $r\\cdot ⟨-1,1,-3⟩=8.$ What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "We need a vector $v=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $r\\cdot ⟨-1,1,-3⟩=8$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨-1,1,-3⟩.$ So, $v⊥n$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,1,-3⟩ & =0 \\\\ -{t}_{1}+{t}_{2}-3{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{2} & ={t}_{1}+3{t}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{1}+3{t}_{3},{t}_{3}⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{1},{t}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nWe are told that the third component of $v$ equals $0.$ So, we must set ${t}_{3}=0.$ \n\nSince $v$ is nonzero, we can pick any ${t}_{1}\\ne 0.$ For example, setting ${t}_{1}=1$, we get\n\n$$\nv=⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{a}{b}=1.$"} :step/id #uuid "0ecb2ec2-22d0-5b28-bc73-c57c70725e45" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423179}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423179 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393043 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "493da2a3-c2b1-5d90-946c-32226dc19e64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨3,-5,-2⟩=⟨-3,4,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393045} {:answer/id #uuid "dc7fa3af-d391-569e-bc17-106f3e9396bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-3,4,-1⟩=⟨3,-5,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393046} {:answer/id #uuid "8fe2b282-7f73-5949-a7f3-cc5ddf1e2530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-3,4,-1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393047} {:answer/id #uuid "949e581b-c362-5470-828e-687788c962cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨3,-5,-2⟩=-27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393048} {:answer/id #uuid "1521f0fb-a511-5bc0-893e-2c6a06cceff9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-3,4,-1⟩=-27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393049}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393049} :answer-field/id #uuid "31ef96c8-9198-5e30-8832-aa55a71dc6d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ca2b2271-1ec0-5a3e-b5ac-687d91668797" :question/math-academy-id "q-70895" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(3,-5,-2)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2-3t \\\\ y=3+4t \\\\ z=4-t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨3,-5,-2⟩.$ Converting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2-3t \\\\ y=3+4t \\\\ z=4-t\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨2,3,4⟩+t\\text{ }{{⟨-3,4,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{v}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe direction vector of the line is $v=⟨-3,4,-1⟩,$ so a normal vector to the plane is \n\n$$\nn=v=⟨-3,4,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-3,4,-1⟩ & =⟨3,-5,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,4,-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-3,4,-1⟩ & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2c8b595f-b1a8-514e-9b95-bb539ab93fc0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423180}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423180 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262855 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0387b95b-ea4c-5624-9fdd-7d44aae31856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨3,1,1⟩=12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259907} {:answer/id #uuid "ad85ebfc-775c-5bdb-a5e6-836a5963e43c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨1,1,5⟩=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261395} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a79415-12c6-5171-8ecf-e701971bbaad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨1,3,1⟩=12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261452} {:answer/id #uuid "d9ba12b0-dcfe-5c43-b282-17a43fb1d275" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨5,1,1⟩=18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261796} {:answer/id #uuid "eb666347-2e39-5891-a53f-4830ffc6eb6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨1,5,1⟩=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261942}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261796} :answer-field/id #uuid "207a2b37-6c4f-5b41-aa59-d8127027f268" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "57b46f7d-b75a-5f5c-87e6-c8948d671ea4" :question/math-academy-id "q-127936" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(3,-2,5)$ and is perpendicular to the line that goes through the points \n$A(-3,0,2)$ and $B(2,1,3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨3,-2,5⟩.$\n\nSince the line  passes through the points $A(-3,0,2)$ and $B(2,1,3)$ we can find a vector parallel to the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨2,1,3⟩-⟨-3,0,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨5,1,1⟩.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨5,1,1⟩ & =⟨3,-2,5⟩\\cdot ⟨5,1,1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨5,1,1⟩ & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "376b1716-abbb-51bb-97e6-b6e06c8b7140"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423181 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423177 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54c2e93d-88d9-55e5-8da7-a7c8857c95a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423171} {:answer/id #uuid "865e5880-5d6c-5eb8-b605-32023bca731a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423172} {:answer/id #uuid "88b7b35b-b8a9-55fa-b177-e46dd3a1176a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423173} {:answer/id #uuid "c899f30d-448a-55d6-a220-50efc27da551" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423174} {:answer/id #uuid "f42a4087-cff6-5d2b-9264-832562c287ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423175}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423174} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed6bca43-1900-5519-94be-29cc1bb55d43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423176}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9509d4b2-8f43-5f25-8917-81a3d5d9b04d" :question/math-academy-id "q-33195" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that contains the point $P(0,1,1)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the position vector of $P$ is $p=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $n=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is normal to the plane.\n\nThe equation of the plane that passes though the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n$ can be given as\n\n$$\n(r-p)\\cdot n=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $p$ is the position vector of $P.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3ecf110e-e7e0-5add-b305-ad1bf6c365c5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423182}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423182 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393029 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4fb71df-948c-5126-86b0-9d79cb4c0fee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393031} {:answer/id #uuid "75ed4713-5dc0-5929-8f1a-ae29368dccf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393032} {:answer/id #uuid "14d6c7a2-54c1-5ce2-9906-4d55cf87f498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393033} {:answer/id #uuid "32938842-cdd7-5118-b60e-0c5d24a27836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393034} {:answer/id #uuid "ea4da8ff-198b-50e0-8319-7a9af39b8918" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393035}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393032} :answer-field/id #uuid "b14d7746-5f36-5ef0-9936-be22c8877f39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393030}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ee04e6aa-94ba-5e6d-a904-4a34aac7a757" :question/math-academy-id "q-70827" :question/problem "A nonzero vector $⟨0,\\,b,\\,c⟩$ is parallel to the plane $r\\cdot ⟨1,2,-1⟩=7.$ What is the value of $\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "We need a vector $v=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $r\\cdot ⟨1,2,-1⟩=7$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨1,2,-1⟩.$ So, $v⊥n$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,2,-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ {t}_{1}+2{t}_{2}-{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{3} & ={t}_{1}+2{t}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=⟨{t}_{1},\\,{t}_{2},\\,{t}_{1}+2{t}_{2}⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{1},{t}_{2}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nWe are told that the first component of $v$ equals $0.$ So, we must set ${t}_{1}=0.$ \n\nSince $v$ is nonzero, we can pick any ${t}_{2}\\ne 0.$ For example, setting ${t}_{2}=1$, we get\n\n$$\nv=⟨0,\\,1,\\,2⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{1}{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "55812343-3b60-5de3-98f2-95ffd7bd0ddb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423178}}] :activity/title "The Vector Equation of a Plane" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423170}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442994} :activity/id #uuid "b4a03ddd-003b-5e8e-82c8-2cc43b396522" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049436 :topic/id #uuid "f5218ca7-a9d3-5ad9-b17a-ee16b6e2b34a" :topic/math-academy-id 1805} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442991 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420153 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b182a09-cc9d-51c4-95fe-e78790ecf72a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420142} {:answer/id #uuid "36cb63bc-ae79-51e4-b5ab-3039c42340e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420143} {:answer/id #uuid "4173b538-e381-5dd5-b0c5-10dc2eaa96c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420144} {:answer/id #uuid "502b671f-b1be-5d29-8f96-08571a87d173" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420145} {:answer/id #uuid "72001519-6957-56f0-b511-f27a9ed3c85f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420146}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420143} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9a0d8dd-871f-5fc8-9699-7a78c5f80aac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3fa29ff6-5ae4-5642-82fd-03b1645e810c" :question/math-academy-id "q-127921" :question/problem "A nonzero vector $⟨0,\\,b,\\,c⟩$ is parallel to the plane $r\\cdot ⟨5,-1,3⟩=27.$ What is the value of $\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "We need a vector $v=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $r\\cdot ⟨5,-1,3⟩=27$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨5,-1,3⟩.$ So, $v⊥n$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨5,-1,3⟩ & =0 \\\\ 5{t}_{1}-{t}_{2}+3{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{2} & =5{t}_{1}+3{t}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=⟨{t}_{1},\\,5{t}_{1}+3{t}_{3},\\,{t}_{3}⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{1},{t}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nWe are told that the first component of $v$ equals $0.$ So, we must set ${t}_{1}=0.$ \n\nSince $v$ is nonzero, we can pick any ${t}_{3}\\ne 0.$ For example, setting ${t}_{3}=1$, we get\n\n$$\nv=⟨0,\\,3,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{b}{c}=3.$"} :step/id #uuid "3458e62b-8854-5734-859a-ce2619927743" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442995}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442992 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387852 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "901763ea-91a4-5ccc-9fd1-7a5abd771549" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨2,2,3⟩=22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387854} {:answer/id #uuid "e471dd7f-cf5c-5449-bdd5-1669c5ee7ff8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨2,3,2⟩=14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387855} {:answer/id #uuid "be245f9d-2743-5776-a695-325f9e3f765e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨6,2,2⟩=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387856} {:answer/id #uuid "e50391f5-f264-5d75-924b-8343f68bda37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨3,2,2⟩=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387857} {:answer/id #uuid "9c146846-53c1-5f64-8789-a2b060a469a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨2,6,2⟩=12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387858}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387856} :answer-field/id #uuid "60a27afd-b25c-559e-94cd-93712349e320" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387853}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "78e0dc1f-5e12-54f0-b00c-492a979933d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-127937" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(2,-1,3)$ and is perpendicular to the line that goes through the points \n$A(-4,1,3)$ and $B(2,3,5).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨2,-1,3⟩.$\n\nSince the line  passes through the points $A(-4,1,3)$ and $B(2,3,5)$ we can find a vector parallel to the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨2,3,5⟩-⟨-4,1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,2,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨6,2,2⟩.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨6,2,2⟩ & =⟨2,-1,3⟩\\cdot ⟨6,2,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨6,2,2⟩ & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "735f1d1f-3dd1-50b8-8d7c-9fcb22c5b1c7"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442993 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338827 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b4cf333-fb31-53e5-a3b2-4d1da8c129ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337143} {:answer/id #uuid "8699f13d-227d-54e9-8db2-f586a65cc4a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337250} {:answer/id #uuid "8ed87010-5adf-5b37-aeca-2e7df330c813" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337317} {:answer/id #uuid "92eae00f-5f08-53b6-b534-72b9e6866cbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337371} {:answer/id #uuid "a20a8aa3-4bd5-5bea-8e7e-61c74a32567a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337501}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337317} :answer-field/id #uuid "557fd6f3-4749-5c34-935d-a0b820019a0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338422}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "22393ec9-8250-54f3-ad30-3ecff187a9da" :question/math-academy-id "q-70814" :question/problem "A nonzero vector $⟨0,\\,b,\\,c⟩$ is parallel to the plane $r\\cdot ⟨1,-2,1⟩=10.$ What is the value of $\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "We need a vector $v=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $r\\cdot ⟨1,-2,1⟩=10$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨1,-2,1⟩.$ So, $v⊥n$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-2,1⟩ & =0 \\\\ {t}_{1}-2{t}_{2}+{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{3} & =2{t}_{2}-{t}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} \\\\ {t}_{2} \\\\ 2{t}_{2}-{t}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{1},{t}_{2}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nWe are told that the first component of $v$ equals $0.$ So, we must set ${t}_{1}=0.$ \n\nSince $v$ is nonzero, we can pick any ${t}_{2}\\ne 0.$ For example, setting ${t}_{2}=1$, we get\n\n$$\nv=⟨0,\\,1,\\,2⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{1}{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "9462b507-c0a1-5898-9afa-bb4186df83ec" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442991}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442994 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120222 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1490998e-fd96-558a-92bf-90c3e1b1d823" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot (\\sqrt{2}i+3j+4k)=2\\sqrt{2}i+7j-6k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442986} {:answer/id #uuid "24bd5c51-ea5f-55c2-b7cc-0a1c6fdaf3e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2}i+7j-6k)=\\sqrt{2}i+3j+4k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442987} {:answer/id #uuid "26e81e0f-00be-5150-9e74-fec4066ead13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2}i+7j-6k)=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442988} {:answer/id #uuid "d8a2f39f-065a-5b9a-9af2-2369637cc071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2}i+7j-6k)=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442989} {:answer/id #uuid "eead13a6-edac-5ccb-bd23-f3ae56dce20e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2}i+7j-6k)=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442990}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442988} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ea9a67f-f999-5059-be26-16de635db5bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117493}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3aea6384-95f5-5566-a595-7a25ca576605" :question/math-academy-id "q-33029" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that contains the point $P(\\sqrt{2},3,4)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=2\\sqrt{2}i+7j-6k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=\\sqrt{2}i+3j+4k,$ and $n=2\\sqrt{2}i+7j-6k$ is normal to the plane.\n\nThe equation of the plane that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n$ can be given as\n\n$$\n(r-p)\\cdot n=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $p$ is the position vector of $P.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2}i+7j-6k) & =(\\sqrt{2}i+3j+4k)\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2}i+7j-6k) \\\\ r\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2}i+7j-6k) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ad4d6328-8534-5210-8c4a-365882fe2824" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442993}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442995 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262853 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1aa825cb-f5f2-5941-b18f-a35a9e8ffaab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-4,-4,3⟩=11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260107} {:answer/id #uuid "394ffd1f-3104-5495-902a-37954445a0c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-4,3,4⟩=12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260385} {:answer/id #uuid "56cda873-6a65-51e4-b130-2eb9c208d8a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨3,4,4⟩=23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260617} {:answer/id #uuid "ce2cec22-6b83-56cf-81ae-8596b434a0ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨4,-3,4⟩=11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261696} {:answer/id #uuid "e6b3d4ad-159b-554b-a0a1-ef2fb9a85ed2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨3,-4,4⟩=23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261900}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261900} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f4a93d8-207b-5307-acbb-b861f6a8320e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9164c795-7f61-5d54-9538-2653e37cb486" :question/math-academy-id "q-127927" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $Q(1,-2,3)$ and is perpendicular to the line that goes through the points \n$A(4,1,-3)$ and $B(7,-3,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨1,-2,3⟩.$\n\nSince the line  passes through the points $A(4,1,-3)$ and $B(7,-3,1)$, we can find a vector that is parallel to the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨7,-3,1⟩-⟨4,1,-3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-4,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨3,-4,4⟩.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-4,4⟩ & =⟨1,-2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-4,4⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-4,4⟩ & =23.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fb8f9fe8-2ed4-5d86-8099-75a35919ccad" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442992}}] :activity/title "The Vector Equation of a Plane" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442985}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106837} :activity/id #uuid "6d587675-28ef-5040-83fe-9aed98369605" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049638 :topic/id #uuid "0748b9c2-fae7-5522-9655-db9e6f1d50c0" :topic/math-academy-id 1347} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106837 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107140 :tutorial/content "Recall that a random variable $X$ is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A random variable can take on one of many values from a set $S,$ called the range or support of $X.$\n\nA **continuous random variable** is a random variable whose set of possible values $S$ is given by an interval of real numbers.\n\nFor example, the height of a randomly selected person could be modeled as a continuous random variable because its possible values form an interval of real numbers. For modeling purposes, we usually assume that the height of a randomly selected person could be *any number* in the real interval $(0,\\infty),$ although we could also restrict this to a finite interval $[a,b]$ if we wanted.\n\nOn the other hand, the result of rolling a $6$-sided die is *not* a continuous random variable because its possible values are $1,2,3,4,5,6,$ which does *not* correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\nPhysical quantities, such as mass, length, area, volume, time, fluid concentration, etc, are usually modeled as continuous variables. \n\n![](images/73/73d1a2c3852734a6221654882ad5421adb565c96c718e91283e5fbc1f26a72c7.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "56f14280-d510-530e-9f12-5e21e8dab7e7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6769 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "8a357dbd-8538-5f7a-a808-d0bb39634099" :step/math-academy-id 41069 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106838}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106838 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105860 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105444 :question/id #uuid "e7c527c9-2dc1-50b2-bbf6-10c1a9d122dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-10837"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ee39554-fd77-5e90-baa8-f9320ffc3398" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048368}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262583} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425875}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Continuous Random Variables Given in Context"} :step/id #uuid "6786cf48-e665-5cb3-ba1f-c245b903bc5f" :step/math-academy-id 41070 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106839}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106839 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107141 :tutorial/content "The **probability density function** (or **pdf**) of a continuous random variable $X$ defined over a set $S$ is a function $f(x)$ such that the probability that $X$ lies in the interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nP(a \\leq X \\leq b) = \\int_a^b f(x) \\, \\textrm dx.\n$$\n\nThus, the probability that $X$ lies between $a$ and $b$ can be interpreted as the area bounded between $f(x)$ and the $x$-axis over the interval $x\\in [a,b],$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/e6/e6c21e69a0dbd15ce32d18c2e74d8fcab0276e82bc55b354eaa5140adff610e6.png)\n\nIn general, for a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function over a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x$ in $S$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nFor example, let the random variable $X$ be the result of randomly selecting a real number between $0$ and $6.$ In this case, the pdf of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 6, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis pdf tells us that any number between $0$ and $6$ has an equal chance of being selected, and no number outside this interval can be selected. \n\nNote that this is a valid pdf because it's always nonnegative, and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}x]}_{0}^{6} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 6]-[\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 0] \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the probability that our randomly selected number is no greater than $3,$ we integrate the pdf over the interval $[0,3]{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0\\le X\\le 3) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}x]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 3]-[\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis result matches our intuition. Since every number has an equal chance of being selected, and $[0,3]$ is half the length of $[0,6],$ we must have $P(0 \\leq X \\leq 3) = \\dfrac12.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "aeeb80c6-8faa-5653-8729-e6dc2c52a2e7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6770 :tutorial/title "Probability Density Functions"} :step/id #uuid "97ac3c7c-a2f7-585b-a8c6-99d14604c5d2" :step/math-academy-id 41073 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106840}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106840 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107142 :tutorial/content "The probability density function of a continuous random variable $X$ is somewhat analogous to the probability *mass* function of a discrete random variable in the sense that it describes the probability distribution of $X.$ \n\nHowever, unlike the case with discrete random variables, probability density is *not* the same as probability! Instead, we can think of a pdf as a tool for computing probabilities for continuous random variables.  \n\nThere are two counterintuitive yet important points to be aware of when it comes to interpreting a pdf:\n\n- *The probability that a continuous random variable takes on any particular value is always equal to $0.$* \n\nContinuing the previous example, the probability that our random variable takes on the value $X=5$ is equal to\n\n$$\nP(X = 5) = P(5 \\leq X \\leq 5) = \\int_5^5 \\dfrac{1}{6} \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nIn general, for any value $a$ and any continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(X = a) = P(a \\leq X \\leq a) = \\int_a^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\n- *The values of a probability density function may be greater than $1.$* \n\nFor example, let $Y$ be the result of randomly selecting a real number between $0$ and $\\dfrac{1}{2}.$ Then, the pdf of $Y$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(y) = 2, \\qquad y \\in \\left[ 0, \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right].\n$$\n\nThis pdf tells us that all numbers between $0$ and $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ are equally likely. Again, $f(y)$ is nonnegative, and \n\n$$\n\\int_0^{1/2} f(y) \\, \\textrm d y = \\int_0^{1/2} 2 \\, \\textrm d y = 1.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(y)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nLastly, note that since the probability that a continuous random variable takes on a particular value is always equal to $0,$ it doesn't matter whether or not we include the endpoints of a particular interval. In other words, for any continuous random variable $X,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(a \\leq X \\leq b) = P(a < X < b) = P(a < X \\leq b) = P(a \\leq X < b) = \\int_a^b f(x) \\, \\textrm dx.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3bac600c-991c-55c0-8e73-72e609d149f5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6771 :tutorial/title "Interpreting Probability Density Functions"} :step/id #uuid "e61652ca-95a2-5028-b7e7-e01a1b6fa9b4" :step/math-academy-id 41074 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106841}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106841 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105861 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105445 :question/id #uuid "295d2025-c958-55e3-a194-5cdb271a510c" :question/math-academy-id "e-10838"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050057}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275472} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376075}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"} :step/id #uuid "39c447c4-4037-52b1-ab5b-5e15228f5477" :step/math-academy-id 41075 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106885}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106885 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105886 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105454 :question/id #uuid "32131e34-3141-51b3-8d26-6cee481f168c" :question/math-academy-id "e-11037"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59bd7df-afa9-5dcf-9dab-fc7f3cf291fa" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278190} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369866}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of an Unknown Constant Given a PDF"} :step/id #uuid "50dd0bf2-ab05-5ddb-8a4b-93e4fad0d2e1" :step/math-academy-id 41931}] :activity/title "Probability Density Functions of Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105985}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125523} :activity/id #uuid "27341ce7-bad3-5506-8e90-5fea806b76fd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049737 :topic/id #uuid "0f66ea93-6fdc-5bbf-826c-7d07b2ef17d7" :topic/math-academy-id 1906} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125341 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125759 :tutorial/content "Let's now compare some properties of addition in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}).$ \nIt turns out that the addition of two matrices in $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is *very* similar to the addition of two vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ See the table below.\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\" width=\"700\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30\"><b></b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"200\"><b>$\\mathbb{R}^2$</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"200\"><b>$\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Addition is Commutative:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A1</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{u}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any matrices $A, B \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}),$ we have $\\color{blue}A+B = B+A$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Addition is Associative:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A2</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{w} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue}(\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v})+\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{u}+(\\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{w})$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any matrices $A, B, C \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}),$ we have $\\color{blue}(A+B) + C = A + (B+C)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Existence of a Unique Zero Element:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A3</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For the zero-vector $\\mathbf{0}$ and any $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue} \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{u}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For the zero-matrix $O$ and any $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}),$ we have $\\color{blue} A + O = A$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Existence of a Unique Opposite Element:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A4</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ we can find the corresponding negative vector $-\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that $\\color{blue} \\mathbf{u} + (-\\mathbf{u}) = \\mathbf{0}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any matrix $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ we can find the corresponding negative matrix $-A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ such that $\\color{blue} A + (-A) = O$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nAnd it's not just matrices that have additive properties similar to those of $\\mathbb R^n.$ Many mathematical structures do. Let's see some more examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "e21e1a72-75b8-5ae3-9b7d-96f08d2ffa86" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3184 :tutorial/title "Addition of Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "f746dfa7-8c4b-5575-86b0-34ab4d9d09e6" :step/math-academy-id 17452 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125342}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125342 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124484 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124297 :question/id #uuid "1a855d24-cb78-5398-89e1-3bfda6907f36" :question/math-academy-id "e-4787"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb09138-45df-5e6c-87af-772980ff7546" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124765} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243917} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243920} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281464} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360469} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360471} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360473} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426551}] :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Vector Addition"} :step/id #uuid "22604921-be1a-5e8f-bdd7-8ac9e59c63a0" :step/math-academy-id 17454 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125522}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125521 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124589 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124400 :question/id #uuid "57bf5fb6-02e9-56a1-9641-342123f45406" :question/math-academy-id "e-8229"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124767} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243955} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281526} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281527} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360475} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360481} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426550}] :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws"} :step/id #uuid "7b0d0289-9feb-5b9b-9cdf-8e8bea357afd" :step/math-academy-id 30785 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125601}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125522 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125832 :tutorial/content "Let's also compare some properties of scalar multiplication in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}).$ It turns out that the multiplication of a matrix in $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ by a scalar is *very* similar to the multiplication of a vector in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ by a real number. See the table below.\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\" width=\"700\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30\"><b></b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"200\"><b>$\\mathbb{R}^2$</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"200\"><b>$\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Invariance Under Multiplication by $1$:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A5</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} 1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{u}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any matrix $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$, we have $\\color{blue} 1 \\cdot A = A$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Scalar Multiplication is Associative:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A6</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\beta \\mathbf{u}) = (\\alpha\\beta) \\mathbf{u}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any matrix $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\beta A) = (\\alpha\\beta) A$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Distributive Laws:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A7</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} (\\alpha + \\beta)\\mathbf{u} = \\alpha \\mathbf{u} + \\beta \\mathbf{u}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any matrix $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$, we have $\\color{blue} (\\alpha + \\beta)A = \\alpha A + \\beta A$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A8</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any number $\\alpha$ and any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{v}) = \\alpha \\mathbf{u} + \\alpha \\mathbf{v}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any number $\\alpha$ and any matrices $A, B \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (A + B) = \\alpha A + \\alpha B$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :tutorial/id #uuid "05cdf1b2-a823-5410-8df1-42fa4da19240" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4934 :tutorial/title "Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws"} :step/id #uuid "605b2c91-28e4-589e-9ea6-93545635ab1c" :step/math-academy-id 30789 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125521}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125523 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125833 :tutorial/content "The structure $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is called a **vector space**. However, many other structures possess properties similar to $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ These other structures can be called vector spaces too. \n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn about some important properties used to construct vector spaces. We begin by discussing what it means for a set to be **closed** under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nAs an example, consider the set $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ of all $2 \\times 2$ matrices with real entries:\n\n![](images/a5/a53ec7e9416ae5eee4a1408df358302567a5aa98a393d6ddcb19e861645d2317.svg)\n\nFor this set of matrices, we can define two operations:\n\n- the addition of two matrices, and\n\n- the multiplication of a matrix by a real number (called a scalar).\n\nNow, notice the following:\n\n- $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is **closed under addition**. This means that the sum of *any* two matrices from $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is also a matrix in $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}).$\n\n- $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is **closed under scalar multiplication**. This means that the product of *any* scalar and *any* matrix from $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is also a matrix in $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}).$\n\nIdentifying that a set is closed under addition and scalar multiplication is an essential first step in determining whether a set can form a vector space." :tutorial/id #uuid "18653f19-b4cf-5f0f-ab39-b1ae33ecdc6b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4935 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "befa8020-1248-59aa-b092-5fd7f5b45c2e" :step/math-academy-id 30790 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125600}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125590 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124628 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124432 :question/id #uuid "ff4fdeaf-bdb3-54d7-b5e9-2f3ad88b55a1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9960"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb02b67c-90d9-5baf-b00e-590e9271d89e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050782} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050979}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278339} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278340} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278341} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281458} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329743} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440655}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets Closed Under Addition and Scalar Multiplication"} :step/id #uuid "78f1d4af-7528-5705-8b7b-224b627efd47" :step/math-academy-id 37506 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125341}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125600 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125860 :tutorial/content "It's important to realize that not all sets are closed under addition and scalar multiplication. Sets not closed under addition and scalar multiplication *cannot* be vector spaces. \n\nFor instance, let $S$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients whose degree is greater than or equal to $5.$  \n\nWe note that $S$ is not closed under addition. To see why this is the case, let's define the polynomials $p(x),q(x)\\in S$ as \n\n$$\np(x) = x^5, \\qquad q(x) = 1-x^5.\n$$\n\nAdding the two polynomials using regular polynomial addition, we get\n\n$$\np(x) + q(x) = x^5 + (1-x^5) = 1\\notin S\n$$\n\nThe sum $p(x) + q(x)$ is not in $S$ because it is a polynomial of degree zero, which is less than $5.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that our set $S$ is *not* closed under addition." :tutorial/id #uuid "b014039e-61b6-58d7-8f35-a72e6153c126" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6230 :tutorial/title "Sets That Are Not Closed"} :step/id #uuid "1402186a-b64d-5406-9767-99815ff7ecc1" :step/math-academy-id 37838 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125590}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125601 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125861 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we discussed several properties exhibited by vectors in $\\mathbb R^n$ and other sets.\nLet's consolidate these properties into a single list.\n\n| **Label** | **Name** | **Description** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| A1 | **Addition is Commutative** | For any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{u}$ |\n| A2 | **Addition is Associative** | For any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{w} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue}(\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v})+\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{u}+(\\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{w})$ |\n| A3 | **Existence of a Unique Zero Element** | For the zero-vector $\\mathbf{0}$ and any $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue} \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{u}$ |\n| A4 | **Existence of a Unique Opposite Element** | For any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ we can find the corresponding negative vector $-\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that $\\color{blue} \\mathbf{u} + (-\\mathbf{u}) = \\mathbf{0}$ |\n| A5 | **Invariance Under Multiplication by $1$** | For any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} 1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{u}$ |\n| A6 | **Scalar Multiplication is Associative** | For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\beta \\mathbf{u}) = (\\alpha\\beta) \\mathbf{u}$ |\n| A7 | **Distributive Laws:** | For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} (\\alpha + \\beta)\\mathbf{u} = \\alpha \\mathbf{u} + \\beta \\mathbf{u}$ |\n| A8 | **Distributive Laws:** | For any number $\\alpha$ and any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{v}) = \\alpha \\mathbf{u} + \\alpha \\mathbf{v}$ |\n\nWe'll meet these properties again in a future lesson, where we'll formally define what we mean by a vector space." :tutorial/id #uuid "72e9ea87-7b8e-5d00-821a-31813b203a2b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6231 :tutorial/title "A Summary of the Addition and Scalar Multiplication Properties of Euclidian Space"} :step/id #uuid "be30c408-bfbc-549b-b14c-3f8724059c1b" :step/math-academy-id 37839}] :activity/title "Introduction to Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124706}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426552} :activity/id #uuid "ad1fad64-3179-5550-8fbd-b2cacb8f6bb0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049737 :topic/id #uuid "0f66ea93-6fdc-5bbf-826c-7d07b2ef17d7" :topic/math-academy-id 1906} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426552 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125219 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e69ab93-723d-5565-8b12-c9c9e92f4afe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342360} {:answer/id #uuid "1766df50-f341-5eb6-89ac-0097e98a3fc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342444} {:answer/id #uuid "6815ad43-217d-52e9-a8da-0aa78e231e03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343124} {:answer/id #uuid "ca897927-fa4a-50ee-97a5-f96cb800a091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344018} {:answer/id #uuid "f42d0555-d3d6-510b-a3a2-94555e1c8df5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344388}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344018} :answer-field/id #uuid "840022d0-492a-56fe-bf5a-c6203cea1ff0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "73f62269-52b9-5341-87d2-165ee03b0821" :question/math-academy-id "q-74700" :question/problem "Let $U$ be the set of polynomials of the form $a{x}^{3},$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The zero-polynomial belongs to $U.$\n\nII. For any $p(x),q(x)\\in U,$ we have $p(x)+q(x)=q(x)+p(x).$\n\nIII. For any $p(x),q(x),r(x)\\in U,$ we have $[p(x)-q(x)]-r(x)=p(x)-[q(x)-r(x)].$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the zero-polynomial can be represented as \n\n$$\n0=0\\cdot {x}^{3}.\n$$\n\n \nHence, $0\\in U.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x),q(x)\\in U,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\np(x)+q(x)=q(x)+p(x)\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is commutative.\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, consider polynomials $p(x)={x}^{3},$ $q(x)=2{x}^{3},$ and $r(x)=3{x}^{3}$ from $U.$ Then: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[p(x)-q(x)]-r(x) & =[{x}^{3}-2{x}^{3}]-3{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}-3{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-4{x}^{3} \\\\ p(x)-[q(x)-r(x)] & ={x}^{3}-[2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{3}] \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-[-{x}^{3}] \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $[p(x)-q(x)]-r(x)\\ne p(x)-[q(x)-r(x)].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "1f027f6f-3170-550b-8b2d-eb37f8a80677" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426556}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426553 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426551 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01b5239c-b3c9-5807-b37b-2a5e43bd141d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426538} {:answer/id #uuid "08624e18-c789-56aa-82f8-d0568d6cacd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426539} {:answer/id #uuid "7ea867be-0628-5646-98c1-28f732a418d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426545} {:answer/id #uuid "96e40a22-66a9-56e3-b03b-cd6284f3fb8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426546} {:answer/id #uuid "d049396a-d59f-556c-a286-aabf743c306b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426547}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426539} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff55dda2-87d0-5c54-864b-9856103ddee8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ca37d526-e95e-5c72-ad4b-d05af7166afb" :question/math-academy-id "q-162394" :question/problem "Let $E$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ that satisfy $f(0)=1.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. The exponential function $f(x)={e}^{x}\\in E$\n\nII. For any $f(x),g(x)\\in E$, we have $f(x)+g(x)\\in E.$\n\nIII. The set $E$ is closed under addition." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that\n\n$$\nf(0)={e}^{0}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\in E.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider the function $f(x)=g(x)={e}^{x}\\in E.$ Then, \n\n$$\n(f+g)(0)={e}^{0}+{e}^{0}=1+1=2\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)+g(x)\\notin E.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $E$ is not closed under addition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "3b196302-0187-5bfa-8880-a9d96466fbba" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426555}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426554 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426550 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0944afe4-0d0b-565f-af88-3dbcade60f38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426540} {:answer/id #uuid "490c90da-97c9-5fee-bb59-bd940c2df35c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426541} {:answer/id #uuid "505c9ae0-7f3e-59b3-8123-543ae49a7508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426542} {:answer/id #uuid "6f7f5023-3192-5bc2-a421-3f0e60a1f07c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426543} {:answer/id #uuid "7a3c0dcd-1ed6-5e80-bd76-4898442d0191" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426544}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426542} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd34e2b9-846d-52d4-a7f0-ad33a82569a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "42098bc3-a0a9-510c-94ee-f777802bb49a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162666" :question/problem "Let $R$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $[\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],$ where $a\\in Z.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. For any $A\\in R,$ we have $(-1)\\cdot A\\in R.$\n\nII. For any $A\\in R,$ we have $2\\cdot A\\in R.$\n\nIII. For any $A\\in R$ and $\\alpha \\in R$, we have $(\\alpha +1)A=\\alpha A+A.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example,the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\in R,$ since $4={2}^{2}$ and $2\\in Z.$ However\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot A=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\notin R,\n$$\n\nbecause a negative number ($-4$) can't be a square.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ from $R.$ Then, we have \n\n$$\n2\\cdot A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\notin R\n$$\n\nbecause the $22$-entry of $2\\cdot A$ is not equal to $1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in R,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +1){a}_{ij}=\\alpha {a}_{ij}+{a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +1)A=\\alpha A+A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "652a43ef-f276-5ff0-8235-02448f8f97ad"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426555 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281525 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2048c5b4-3996-5c47-9a17-90d26d347fc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278889} {:answer/id #uuid "2ef2145c-b1f4-56c4-9959-10d10c087ace" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279000} {:answer/id #uuid "762200d2-8ac3-54b0-a844-d5ae31f4789b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279645} {:answer/id #uuid "dddcd65b-9ce6-50ce-9228-6bcf5cea7db0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280548} {:answer/id #uuid "e26ca2e7-67dd-5dc7-a4ef-404b70019c20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280593}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280548} :answer-field/id #uuid "9eae0065-06b1-55e5-93fa-33a43fe9d0d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ff69a5c1-505a-5719-b6b9-7ec69e0931ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-162664" :question/problem "Let $Q$ be the set of all functions of the form $f(x)=a{x}^{2},$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. There is a function $f(x)\\in Q,$ such that $(-1)\\cdot f(x)=f(x).$\n\nII. For any $f(x)\\in Q$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).$\n\nIII. For any $f(x),g(x)\\in Q$ and $\\alpha \\in R,$ we have $\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider the null function $f(x)=0=0{x}^{2}\\in Q.$ Then,\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot f(x)=(-1)\\cdot 0=0=f(x).\n$$\n\nHence, $(-1)\\cdot f(x)=f(x).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any function $f(x)\\in Q$ and any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any functions $f(x),g(x)\\in Q$ and any fixed $x\\in R,$ the results of $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} :step/id #uuid "a1742ae5-ae23-562a-91d7-bbd1cf427a86" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426554}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426556 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360475 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e019c0c-c668-5ed1-a867-43f40e187002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358011} {:answer/id #uuid "93241e3f-6a8f-5ad8-bfac-2641dcb5f85f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359039} {:answer/id #uuid "ab1ff7cd-a746-57e7-b6af-1dcad05500df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359224} {:answer/id #uuid "ba745c6e-4d9f-526e-b1ed-68947cdeb6e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359373} {:answer/id #uuid "d0fa2cf0-d079-54dc-a60a-17daf11e5c53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359581}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358011} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fea600c-8657-5d8b-912c-ccccb2894d70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d30a6a74-1882-58de-a0e8-caf74485928f" :question/math-academy-id "q-94700" :question/problem "Let $R$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],$ where $a,b\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\nI. For any $A\\in R,$ we have $1\\cdot A=A.$\n\nII. For any $A\\in R,$ we have $-1(-1\\cdot A)=-A.$\n\nIII. For any $A\\in R$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R$, we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )A=\\alpha A+\\beta A.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in R$ with real entries, we have\n\n$$\n1\\cdot {a}_{ij}={a}_{ij}\n$$\n\nand, hence,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot A=A.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ from $R.$  Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1(-1\\cdot A) & =-1(-1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =-1([\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot 1 & (-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot 0 & (-1)\\cdot 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =-1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-1) & (-1)\\cdot (-2) \\\\ (-1)\\cdot 0 & (-1)\\cdot (-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =A\\ne -A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements III is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in R,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta ){a}_{ij}=\\alpha {a}_{ij}+\\beta {a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )A=\\alpha A+\\beta A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "ea8e9bb5-f8d7-5d3d-83dd-b2ae2b6c3647" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426553}}] :activity/title "Introduction to Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426537}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153088} :activity/id #uuid "de6336c1-f5b4-5304-9fdd-384cbc487c0b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050032 :topic/id #uuid "277bf882-73ab-515e-84e5-4f7920acb235" :topic/math-academy-id 2549} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153075 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152169 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151964 :question/id #uuid "3a4c4912-e478-5332-b088-0df7ac4e8c42" :question/math-academy-id "e-6578"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "101e0c22-fbb4-5865-af5f-d669e2488d09" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152637} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152638} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335793} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335794} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335795} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368764} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400562} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404058}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Linear ODEs Using the Superposition Principle"} :step/id #uuid "230b3a14-dd22-5c8a-94c5-5be6dc2128a7" :step/math-academy-id 24210} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153088 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153364 :tutorial/content "Recall that a second-order linear ODE can always be expressed in the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x).\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that the equation is *homogeneous* if $f(x) = 0$ for all $x{:}$\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y =0\n$$\n\nThe **superposition principle** states that if the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are solutions to a linear homogeneous differential equation, then the function \n\n$$\ny(x) = y_1(x) + y_2(x)\n$$\n\nis also a solution to the equation.\n\nTo illustrate, consider the following homogeneous differential equation:\n\n$$\ny'' + 16y = 0\n$$\n\n \n\nTwo solutions of this equation are\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = \\cos 4x, \\qquad y_2(x) = \\sin 4x.\n$$\n\nWe can easily check that these are solutions by substituting them into the equation and verifying that they result in true statements:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{''}+16{y}_{1} & =0 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}4x)^{''}+16\\operatorname{cos}4x & =0 \\\\ (-4\\operatorname{sin}4x)^{'}+16\\operatorname{cos}4x & =0 \\\\ -16\\operatorname{cos}4x+16\\operatorname{cos}4x & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {y}_{2}^{''}+16{y}_{2} & =0 \\\\ (\\operatorname{sin}4x)^{''}+16\\operatorname{sin}4x & =0 \\\\ (4\\operatorname{cos}4x)^{'}+16\\operatorname{sin}4x & =0 \\\\ -16\\operatorname{sin}4x+16\\operatorname{sin}4x & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the equation is linear and homogeneous, the superposition principle guarantees that the sum\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}4x+\\operatorname{sin}4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis also a solution to the differential equation. \n\nWe can check that this is a solution to the equation by substituting (keeping the order consistent with our definition):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{''}+16y & =0 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}4x+\\operatorname{sin}4x)^{''}+16(\\operatorname{cos}4x+\\operatorname{sin}4x) & =0 \\\\ (-4\\operatorname{sin}4x+4\\operatorname{cos}4x)^{'}+16(\\operatorname{cos}4x+\\operatorname{sin}4x) & =0 \\\\ (-16\\operatorname{cos}4x-16\\operatorname{sin}4x)+16\\operatorname{cos}4x+16\\operatorname{sin}4x & =0 \\\\ 0=0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** The superposition principle does *not* extend to the products or quotients of the known solutions. For example, the functions\n\n$$\n\\cos4x\\sin4x, \\quad \\sin^24x, \\quad \\dfrac{\\sin4x}{\\cos 4x},\n$$\n\nare *not* solutions to our original differential equation." :tutorial/id #uuid "c370ce9c-1004-553d-8faf-cd8c882feea2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4087 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a1ef948a-d0ad-5a62-bffa-6701e75912a8" :step/math-academy-id 24527 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153109}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153109 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152192 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151996 :question/id #uuid "7e533704-ef66-5bab-b151-83345bce320b" :question/math-academy-id "e-6865"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bc95a3-4e0c-5487-8427-31cee0ecf80b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050677}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152634} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152809} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266037} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348278} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348285} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348296}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Sum Rule"} :step/id #uuid "f01e7386-b41a-5337-b62d-62077f6bdb02" :step/math-academy-id 25425 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153115}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153110 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152193 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151997 :question/id #uuid "c4c3ed32-67c8-56a3-9855-4bfa2696bfc9" :question/math-academy-id "e-6866"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266039} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367345}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Constant Multiple Rule"} :step/id #uuid "b8ccaf97-01ff-5727-8fc0-3612aa7915d2" :step/math-academy-id 25426 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153116}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153115 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153383 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the second-order homogeneous ODE\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = 0.\n$$\n\nThe superposition principle also states that if $y_1(x)$ is a solution to the equation, then the function \n\n$$\ny(x) = cy_1(x)\n$$\n\nis also a solution to the equation, where $c$ is a constant. \n\nTo illustrate, let's once again consider the following linear differential equation:\n\n$$\ny'' + 16y = 0\n$$\n\n \n\nRecall that\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = \\cos 4x, \\qquad y_2(x) = \\sin 4x\n$$\n\n are solutions to this equation. So, according to the superposition principle, the functions\n\n$$\n6\\cos 4x, \\quad -\\sin 4x, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\pi}{e}\\cos 4x\n$$\n\nare all solutions to the ODE.\n\nWe can check that these are indeed solutions to the equation by substituting. To illustrate, let's verify the solution $6\\cos 4x = 6y_1(x)$ by evaluating the left-hand side of this equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{''}+16y & =(6\\operatorname{cos}4x)^{''}+16(6\\operatorname{cos}4x) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}4x)^{''}+6\\cdot 16(\\operatorname{cos}4x) \\\\  & =6\\cdot ((\\operatorname{cos}4x)^{''}+16\\operatorname{cos}4x) \\\\  & =6\\cdot ((-4\\operatorname{sin}4x)^{'}+16\\operatorname{cos}4x) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (-16\\operatorname{cos}4x+16\\operatorname{cos}4x) \\\\  & =6\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** This idea extends to *scalar* (or constant) multiples only. For example, the function $x\\cos 4x$ is *not* a solution to our ODE." :tutorial/id #uuid "3d8a4098-396f-59ef-bbc5-30f0f4342942" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4198 :tutorial/title "Constant Multiples of Solutions"} :step/id #uuid "b6ebe766-97a0-541f-b401-9aec830d1c73" :step/math-academy-id 25485 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153110}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153116 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153384 :tutorial/content "In its most general form, the **superposition principle** states that any linear combination (or \"superposition\") of solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation is also a solution. More precisely:\n\n> *If the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c_1$ and $c_2$ are constants, then $y(x) = c_1 y_1(x) + c_2 y_2(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.*\n\nNote that the superposition principle follows immediately from the definition of a linear differential operator $L.$ Recall that every homogeneous equation can be written as\n\n$$\nL(y) = 0.\n$$\n\nNow, let $y = c_1 y_1(x) + c_2y_2(x).$ Then, by the linearity of $L,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(y) & =L({c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x)) \\\\  & =L({c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x))+L({c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x)) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}L({y}_{1}(x))+{c}_{2}L({y}_{2}(x)) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\cdot 0+{c}_{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0+0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose we're told that the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' - y = 0\n$$\n\nhas two solutions\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = e^x \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad y_2(x) = e^{-x}.\n$$\n\nThen, according to the superposition principle, any linear combination\n\n$$\ny(x) = c_1 e^x + c_2 e^{-x}\n$$\n\nis also a solution.\n\n- For instance, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =2{y}_{1}(x)-\\frac{1}{3}{y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis also a solution because it takes the form $c_1 e^x + c_2 e^{-x}$ with $c_1 = 2$ and $c_2 = -\\dfrac{1}{3}.$ $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\n- However, we *cannot* conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={x}^{2}{y}_{1}(x)+\\operatorname{sin}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{e}^{x}+\\operatorname{sin}(x){e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a solution because $x^2$ and $\\sin(x)$ are not constants. $\\quad \\color{red}\\times$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4e57a559-0bde-5f71-b99f-93c685361eb1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4199 :tutorial/title "The Superposition Principle in Its Most General Form"} :step/id #uuid "d88e8300-d051-5777-b95d-2efea037150a" :step/math-academy-id 25486 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153075}}] :activity/title "The Superposition Principle" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152307}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130023} :activity/id #uuid "c092012f-6280-5531-b39f-09c65d20ea5d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050822 :topic/id #uuid "6961ffb7-2e37-5f54-ac39-0331f4517bbc" :topic/math-academy-id 1979} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130023 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130371 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the region that lies between the curves ${\\color{red}y=f(x)}$ and ${\\color{blue}y=g(x)}$ over a closed interval $[a,b]$ on the $x$-axis, as shown below.\n\n![](images/a4/a45abf7904382a16daaa38ff617a9487b5c274cf65ef44f89e7feb0c32c496e0.png)\n\nThis region is an example of a **type I region**. Generally, a type I region is any region bounded by two vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b,$ and two curves $y=g(x)$ and $y=f(x),$ where $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions. \n\nUsing set notation, a type I region can be written as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: a \\leq x  \\leq b, \\quad g(x) \\leq y \\leq f(x) \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nIn a type I region, any vertical line drawn through the region should intersect the region in a *single* interval, as shown below.\n\n![](images/f7/f7282efbe2c9244c77ce6bbbeca047ee3ab29dd2fc90e0d2440c519328cb5973.png)\n\nBy contrast, the following region is *not* a type I region since there is a vertical line whose intersection with the region results in two *separate* line segments. In other words, the vertical line enters and leaves the region more than once.\n\n![](images/a2/a26c7588e297a8af35f1e709210596a94f3fb4bd84e8237dc1462f48b961630e.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "2658cc5e-d169-5ff8-871c-4ccaab428eb7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3854 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0b518252-fe1c-57d7-bc6f-6a00d3addac9" :step/math-academy-id 22589 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130024}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130024 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129167 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128986 :question/id #uuid "9cbb7321-753d-5253-8b64-072e3cc227bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-6101"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64d342be-ebaf-5e94-b641-ea8ce6f14c12" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049051} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050641}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129837} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435896}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Type I Regions"} :step/id #uuid "1b6899de-a336-56a9-809a-31cc01aeebb8" :step/math-academy-id 22590 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130025}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130025 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130372 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the region that lies between the curves ${\\color{red}x=f(y)}$ and ${\\color{blue}x=g(y)}$ over a closed interval $[a,b]$ on the $y$-axis, as shown below.\n\n![](images/cf/cf2a2a41d63f21b279215fd1aa8d7361eb638e831b970bb3de861673f67612e6.png)\n\nThis region is an example of a **type II region**. In general, a type II region is any region that is bounded by two horizontal lines $y=a$ and $y=b,$ and two curves $x=g(y)$ and $x=f(y),$ where $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nUsing set notation, a type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: a \\leq y  \\leq b, \\quad g(y) \\leq x \\leq f(y) \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nIn a type II region, any horizontal line drawn through the region should intersect the region in a *single* interval, as shown below.\n\n![](images/56/56019e8cc2b1146b99fa6f6532356281fdb847c342c25c63f843ddee4f8cb197.png)\n\nBy contrast, the following region is *not* a type II region since there is a horizontal line whose intersection with the region results in two *separate* line segments. In other words, the horizontal line enters and leaves the region more than once.\n\n![](images/a7/a7c1ead1274250f7184b132eff58a3028ce648715dcbfa7c7046077fd4e680ef.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "d089f833-c09a-5229-9be0-8cffa2510d60" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3855 :tutorial/title "Type II Regions"} :step/id #uuid "4858561d-380d-50d9-901d-e33ce219f2d6" :step/math-academy-id 22591 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130026}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130026 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129168 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128987 :question/id #uuid "cce0a7b2-7b25-5da6-8aa2-cbe8a40cd522" :question/math-academy-id "e-6102"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85335de6-e1dc-5a2f-b30a-806627759030" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339049} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339051} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375321} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389594} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435897}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Type II Regions"} :step/id #uuid "952b663b-02bc-55c3-b391-c854df6de6fb" :step/math-academy-id 22592 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130027}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130027 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130373 :tutorial/content "In general, a given region can be type I, type II, both, or neither.\n\nFor example, let's consider the region in the coordinate plane bounded between the circle ${\\color{blue}x^2+y^2=2}$ and the lines ${\\color{red}x=-1},$ ${\\color{red}x=1},$ ${\\color{red}y=-1}$, and ${\\color{red}y=1},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/db/db43502c2b096c912ac38d297c3a06e7dcbf4945af6fbc7f5e365a40d11fcde8.png)\n\nThere is a vertical line (for instance, the $y$-axis) whose intersection with the region results in two separate line segments. In other words, the vertical line enters and leaves the region more than once. So, this region *cannot* be a type I region.\n\n![](images/dc/dc1dca3b3015da15f554d95dae7add65aeee0b83a0075ec5295716a3e86d71f5.png)\n\nSimilarly, there is a horizontal line (for instance, the $x$-axis) whose intersection with the region results in two separate line segments. In other words, the horizontal line enters and leaves the region more than once. So, this region *cannot* be a type II region.\n\n![](images/82/82720f4b69fc3e63bbf54cb22d60299035faf91a6021f09d41e9f62dad571581.png)\n\nIn conclusion, this region is *neither* type I nor type II.\n\nLet's now look at an example that is *both* type I and type II." :tutorial/id #uuid "09369aae-8943-5475-9739-20e6619b16d7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3856 :tutorial/title "Regions that are Neither Type I Nor Type II"} :step/id #uuid "eafe2c8b-e7b1-59e6-9468-2c731ee86e98" :step/math-academy-id 22593 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130028}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130028 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129169 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128988 :question/id #uuid "cd9ce5bf-1663-5110-8aaa-8f0f47d7d30a" :question/math-academy-id "e-6103"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7948909f-8dcb-5cc0-a00f-f505a41e4004" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048598} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049570} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049699} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050294}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129841} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129842} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403564} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403647} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406982}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Region as Type I, Type II, Both, or Neither"} :step/id #uuid "3b78b691-c694-59d1-b1ef-5ea8a77ad679" :step/math-academy-id 22594}] :activity/title "Type I and II Regions in Two-Dimensional Space" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129327}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406984} :activity/id #uuid "3da669c9-a862-59b7-84e5-f7ce7ac62942" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050822 :topic/id #uuid "6961ffb7-2e37-5f54-ac39-0331f4517bbc" :topic/math-academy-id 1979} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406983 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393194 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5bce626-d9bb-5dbb-905a-7daed41e086c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/a2/a216b288b70301fb14b8f1219b909edb69ab77b08c29460b5522abc2025ab494.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393196} {:answer/id #uuid "c9d47ae6-371d-5f7f-a924-edcd6ec62dd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393197} {:answer/id #uuid "693ea9c7-f1ff-5a1c-b288-3ea683ff953d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/20/205fdc7a29c94d36b5bd8847a2600c41d7e3fd2ac0fd92947dddcb241bdae012.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393198} {:answer/id #uuid "349c2643-543b-501a-b8d3-7af986d4a0af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/25/254691f56d0586b2cbfd75a3fce34ecc1062b0f0fde538d3885860ed1b71555c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393199} {:answer/id #uuid "d7836927-19a4-54a2-aabf-fc9fd48ada96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ed/edbcc3bcef921ef736f9bd31bec56f69736c632bf835cf4c9c1c46026fda8083.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393200}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393196} :answer-field/id #uuid "7683d677-08f6-57b3-b924-04b4d1ba5f5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393195}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "13be6924-14b7-5b8e-8469-18a97723bd20" :question/math-academy-id "q-72120" :question/problem "Which of the following is a type II region in two-dimensional space?" :question/worked-solution "A type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,g(y)\\le x\\le f(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nA type II region has the property that a horizontal line drawn through the region intersects the region in a single interval only.\n\nAmong the given options, the only type II region is the following.\n\n![](images/e4/e472070f17c47e78024217ed750690b88de87f3849818368dda1dde5d91d90d4.png)"} :step/id #uuid "0868f274-6d6e-5256-97a9-564c4f1a8169" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406985}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406984 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339049 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "099efdac-6222-5744-b3c1-1dad27d41b85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336117} {:answer/id #uuid "2f51b9b3-9edb-5ad5-a10c-52cfdfd9e751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/6c/6ccd325f24c9550f82a7063623db1cae0212a07f1c938b882c6707d35a6eee29.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336484} {:answer/id #uuid "5a4f7dba-2306-55b0-ad53-f7348ae38b3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/5a/5a14be084ba22e33b636465156276fc489904a650077612c15fdec3634f8d902.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336855} {:answer/id #uuid "d8f500b5-47d1-5c54-82d7-03d7175ff4bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/5e/5e41509dcb689a5f16822eea9b8700171a74124789e12990ee4fd292d52aa2ea.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337919} {:answer/id #uuid "f39c32b9-aa95-5261-a245-c6851bf03b8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/81/81b206d34bfa12b8623329616368ea3e6a978cf7963dbe5f09b23cafffb76dae.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338160}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336484} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0492a08-2d29-5eca-af88-ac3ff00ea955" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "efbcfea6-0f26-55f3-9790-4f12e94364d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-72049" :question/problem "Which of the following is a type II region in two-dimensional space?" :question/worked-solution "A type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,g(y)\\le x\\le f(y)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nA type II region has the property that a horizontal line drawn through the region intersects the region in a single interval only.\n\nAmong the given options, the only type II region is the following.\n\n![](images/7c/7c159c025f0b7ae4bfa8984f637a492cda21ec2dd39e87be724324e1334c06a3.png)"} :step/id #uuid "11bc950f-28ab-5ada-8524-78d17fbdb0fc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406983}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406985 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373174 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0747cf82-408b-58c2-b5d7-dcefd0ed68ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le 1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435869} {:answer/id #uuid "17567c73-9403-53e0-b46b-a5ceadd85060" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435871} {:answer/id #uuid "429ef4b8-fbc0-567d-af67-084931824074" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le y\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435875} {:answer/id #uuid "4b704cda-a7e0-5c8b-9cf6-66429dfd1ade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{2},\\,0\\le x\\le 1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435876} {:answer/id #uuid "a709f16c-6935-5b16-8b38-88908d6454cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,y\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435884}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435884} :answer-field/id #uuid "257fac2f-8a02-59c7-aca1-ef19cbb1795a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373175}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ef441fce-64e9-5f90-b9fb-0d44af6b333e" :question/math-academy-id "q-72143" :question/problem "![](images/76/768046952484d181a404486547db84882424d98da4f677254484e1083580a39b.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the shaded region above as a type II region in two-dimensional space?" :question/worked-solution "A type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,g(y)\\le x\\le f(y)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type II region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $y:$\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{y}\n$$\n\nThe function $x=y$ is already written as a function of $y.$\n\nNotice that the region lies between the horizontal lines $y=0$ and $y=1.$ So, we have $0\\le y\\le 1.$\n\nWe draw a horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left function $g(y)$ and the right function $f(y).$ A horizontal line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type II region.\n\n![](images/d5/d55cb7ea67d9865368c744577bb4a4b22a3635e8e62869ee65e945612d6c0e98.png)\n\nThe horizontal line enters the region through the lower function $x=y$ and leaves through the upper function $x=\\sqrt{y}.$ So, we have $y\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,y\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y}\\}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1c227960-e1d3-5f07-b75a-31b153d00a8a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406987}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406986 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339070 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d5dfdf6-e50a-544c-a082-757d6a3cc45e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336750} {:answer/id #uuid "6f3a5eba-d4b1-5a73-b36e-ccfbf5537a92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337017} {:answer/id #uuid "71d9509f-880d-594a-ad0c-8f134a4a17bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337050} {:answer/id #uuid "87c90cf0-1d0a-5ecb-9f7d-4e9022a09814" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337256} {:answer/id #uuid "fc897d08-7b5c-5946-a0d6-80162b7a9c92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338252}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337256} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dc16394-01fd-51be-9be9-07cf0cfe3cbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f581ab20-43e5-59a8-ac58-914378a7361e" :question/math-academy-id "q-72186" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the parabola $y=4-{x}^{2}$ and the line $y=2-|x|?$\n\nI. It is a type I region\n\nII. It is a type II region\n\nIII. It's neither a type I nor a type II region" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/6f/6f35161e7efbd53c9ef558ffe88d8e046771128965a9fdd02143ae37a9b130ea.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- Any vertical line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,2-|x|\\le y\\le 4-{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type I region.\n\n- The horizontal line $y=1$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type II region.\n\n![](images/76/769a521cd6b05ecf05052da382e40295b26be66fe56e4eff7c6940c9032f2f0b.png)\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."} :step/id #uuid "52cd1fa6-d29c-562d-9d88-cade3cec5a66"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406987 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406982 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11ecb354-bca2-5a72-beb3-0f34f2bbf979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406976} {:answer/id #uuid "20731cc6-c38c-5d60-ad49-b4b85b44d30a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406977} {:answer/id #uuid "2548e166-c6fa-5f0c-9863-ed09f3c8467c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406978} {:answer/id #uuid "4e41d7a4-75e3-58cd-839a-7c89df99b321" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406979} {:answer/id #uuid "b32afa94-923e-5681-a943-6f823eddc017" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406980}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406979} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ba14fe2-fe7c-5c94-b156-885bef914d7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0d779a1b-dcae-59f0-822c-7860f9483ab7" :question/math-academy-id "q-72160" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the curves $y={x}^{3}$ and $y=\\sqrt[3]{x},$ in the first quadrant?\n\n- It is a type I region\n- It is a type II region\n- It's neither a type I nor a type II region" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{3}\\, & ⟹\\,x=\\sqrt[3]{y} \\\\ y=\\sqrt[3]{x}\\, & ⟹\\,x={y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/89/899357795c6d5b81d3cb619d71493ad787645697fe1f2fcecda78c1b875f388e.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- Any vertical line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{3}\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{x}}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type I region.\n\n- Any horizontal line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,{y}^{3}\\le x\\le \\sqrt[3]{y}}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type II region.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."} :step/id #uuid "fd242e61-69b5-5bfd-bc83-d7379d3f45c8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406986}}] :activity/title "Type I and II Regions in Two-Dimensional Space" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406975}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429979} :activity/id #uuid "d57582b7-233f-54ba-bba8-bc7760d18c20" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050822 :topic/id #uuid "6961ffb7-2e37-5f54-ac39-0331f4517bbc" :topic/math-academy-id 1979} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429976 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429975 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "177be529-dbfe-5550-a65b-a03b4c722a7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le {e}^{x-1},\\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)+1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429962} {:answer/id #uuid "69b6c086-4549-55a7-9eba-d0041a274109" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le e,\\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)+1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429966} {:answer/id #uuid "ad13df4f-7bd9-5a03-86d7-02175fdb12da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le e,\\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)+1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429968} {:answer/id #uuid "c5dacd0b-ebeb-5ba4-b6db-4ab0dd2f71e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le {e}^{x-1},\\,1\\le x\\le 2\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429970} {:answer/id #uuid "dfdb17ab-530e-5006-a6b1-b68bfc5121a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le {e}^{x-1},\\,0\\le x\\le 2\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429971}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429966} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1839b45-678e-5716-ac62-7bfdda74ad68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429972}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5cace0f5-d8d3-5cc8-93c4-64d577bf2d3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-72344" :question/problem "![](images/40/4066b07ace047e9a7ce813ed70dd6648649a192d648ed810fa96b5146d2c5581.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the shaded region above as a type II region in two-dimensional space?" :question/worked-solution "A type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,g(y)\\le x\\le f(y)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type II region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $y:$\n\n$$\ny={e}^{x-1}\\,⟹\\,x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)+1\n$$\n\nThe function $x=0$ is already written as a function of $y.$\n\nNotice that the region lies between the horizontal lines $y=1$ and $y=e.$ So, we have $1\\le y\\le e.$\n\nWe draw a horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left function $g(y)$ and the right function $f(y).$ A horizontal line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type II region.\n\n![](images/68/680842905f6e308d3c438063769bde9854401a300371452d1c0735bb7d272a02.png)\n\nThe horizontal line enters the region through the lower function $x=0$ and leaves through the upper function $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)+1.$ So, we have $0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)+1.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le e,\\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)+1\\}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3390ee50-86eb-5461-9633-3fe230269903" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429978}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429977 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339069 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17be251d-7119-505a-822a-7b4f4a540bc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336254} {:answer/id #uuid "28248254-c521-555f-a8eb-3698fd411628" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336409} {:answer/id #uuid "54358dc3-968d-51c2-b7a6-0a9e65676557" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336809} {:answer/id #uuid "559f9ad0-2178-5631-a30c-df72edf7e3d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336818} {:answer/id #uuid "55dfee5e-aee0-54ba-a2ef-8bb2f5c930f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336821}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336254} :answer-field/id #uuid "acf0a001-ed03-592c-9465-79a8c55ab0a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338594}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0bcd8034-22f3-5298-b8d3-b69cdcbc84fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-72185" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region inside the circumference ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1?$\n\nI. It is a type I region\n\nII. It is a type II region\n\nIII. It's neither a type I nor a type II region" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\\, & ⟹\\,x=\\pm \\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\\, & ⟹\\,y=\\pm \\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/09/0960063edcce8ccd55dc2f77147c4a4982645c180e8b998d7e520c65131ea045.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- Any vertical line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type I region.\n\n- Any horizontal line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,-\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type II region.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."} :step/id #uuid "4eb88ece-6f0a-54bd-b308-acf69dcf4cf6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429980}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429978 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389594 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e353de59-45ed-5d3e-a3c7-c62ccb8bfee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/32/32e6ed07f3229c8e6016d12dd35c42597888662fbc909f53bc07e495a73958e8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389596} {:answer/id #uuid "189f5878-608c-523c-8af3-61b336e7fea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389597} {:answer/id #uuid "cae29720-9bdf-5675-b7dc-87fb52dc9fc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/36/36275eef6e634c83c614626bc2af6b97cd5bafddc8c02b64ba2a1aa7ea983317.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389598} {:answer/id #uuid "b6e89d64-16cb-5f6e-924f-288db18c0bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/de/de643820e7690a10eae2e7de2e2cb974700996b36130af4f45c446640b49dcc4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389599} {:answer/id #uuid "a2e82089-b886-5a96-8c4e-a2c7d256121e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/5a/5ac45c6757e187796674ca4beda4ff73c60835b401df302bfd8a91fa5c98b68c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389600}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389599} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e761f31-f26b-54c2-90ef-d2fbb7506ab7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389595}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2da77bc4-04e1-56ef-a7c8-2c62ea7852fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-168140" :question/problem "Which of the following is a type II region in two-dimensional space?" :question/worked-solution "A type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,g(y)\\le x\\le f(y)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nA type II region has the property that a horizontal line drawn through the region intersects the region in a single interval only.\n\nAmong the given options, the only type II region is the following.\n\n![](images/1b/1bee7870760a7c267ca83806f04b3fa8b933891fa5e5524ce76ee09f2c7c98e0.png)"} :step/id #uuid "89758ef5-24c5-59a5-a86a-6a96520e9970"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429979 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429974 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ad9fabe-6ac2-5a64-ad33-5fdd7127093f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429963} {:answer/id #uuid "1d70f350-c29f-598f-92ec-fbca126bbc5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/78/781f679784282a55e4ff6b7a5537047ef2f45c8346cc9d1ed341407ecb8f8a38.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429964} {:answer/id #uuid "52b32bb1-058f-52fc-8bbd-90d501685805" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ba/ba9994e94193fb25ac13c50e22e17c571ef563420325a02b593a37c4634e3998.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429965} {:answer/id #uuid "795e2b60-0fa8-5665-b329-1cbd1b3e42fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/69/69de9afdd158f32add63cc93e37b12ed0e3a80fcb5b1cb04cb88fb493c9ca723.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429967} {:answer/id #uuid "b347940c-aaa8-5068-a270-5cc37376373d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/70/70662d02852c7299ab1c040362664de79de8bc51689b0611796f63a737412693.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429969}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429967} :answer-field/id #uuid "e615de10-cf5a-5071-a58d-94a8d37e22e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "033e3725-7b31-599b-85d8-6c6f830b966d" :question/math-academy-id "q-72042" :question/problem "Which of the following is a type I region in two-dimensional space?" :question/worked-solution "A type I region can be written as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le x\\le b,\\,g(x)\\le y\\le f(x)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nA type I region has the property that a vertical line drawn through the region intersects the region in a single interval only.\n\nAmong the given options, the only type I region is the following.\n\n![](images/00/00654c32dbc74614de9b6fdd0f39a5f7d4ecb6ccca0c642ab7ca4a2a8cfe1d89.png)"} :step/id #uuid "8bd0027a-2407-5324-8c03-911f21cc41f4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429977}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429980 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339071 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05b81e3f-060b-5845-a303-b88224da1581" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336078} {:answer/id #uuid "46f070d3-ed05-5034-af82-a9adf57c60b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336690} {:answer/id #uuid "4deab650-a626-55f2-b9af-f7b4ccb3ee34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336755} {:answer/id #uuid "5e1230df-1cda-5e95-8f8f-00f39e16c196" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336882} {:answer/id #uuid "cf98decd-9264-56e3-850f-4cfdc80409db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337849}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336690} :answer-field/id #uuid "08cff232-9d15-5600-89b1-e7cd0fac0002" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b03842bf-e94f-5526-903d-de5a2a3bccca" :question/math-academy-id "q-72187" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the circles ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2$ and ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1?$\n\nI. It is a type I region\n\nII. It is a type II region\n\nIII. It's neither a type I nor a type II region" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/28/28290c5530efd27a979f4284481ca7e0384342d7d426dde0ab36894a0d77a166.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- The vertical line $x=0.5$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type I region.\n\n![](images/30/3007cffb725faee44d2a06ad077eec630791b1a94c373a323f62d0d721c87207.png)\n\n- The horizontal line $y=0.5$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type II region.\n\n![](images/38/383f88668435556d3a856cf852caf548b1f1bf3a90d50f9d85d7968188984804.png)\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."} :step/id #uuid "ad81c404-9fb0-5596-9d64-58c400c8f6c0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429976}}] :activity/title "Type I and II Regions in Two-Dimensional Space" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429961}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101591} :activity/id #uuid "0674df03-7c13-5be8-b206-8215c54adc2f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050898 :topic/id #uuid "6f6a1e18-10c1-5fb2-9f41-d2b49cf97ce7" :topic/math-academy-id 1161} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101591 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186102029 :tutorial/content "Let's think about how to calculate an integral like $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac {\\ln x} {x} \\,\\textrm d x.$\n\nNotice that this integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac {\\ln x} {x} \\,\\textrm d x = \\int \\underbrace{\\ln x}_{u} \\cdot\\overbrace{\\dfrac {1} {x}}^{u'} \\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe've seen integrals like this before, where the integrand consists of a function $u(x)$ multiplied by its derivative $u'(x).$ Such integrals can be solved by substitution.\n\nSo, we let $u = \\ln x,$ which gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= \\dfrac 1 x\\quad   \\Longrightarrow \\quad \\dfrac 1 x \\,\\textrm{d}x={\\textrm{d}u}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we can calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n       \\int \\dfrac{\\ln x}{x}\\, \\textrm{d}x \t&=\\int \\ln x\\cdot \\dfrac {1} {x} \\,\\textrm d x \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t& = \\int u \\, \\textrm{d} u \\[5pt]\n                                                                &=  \\dfrac{1}{2}u^2  +C \\[5pt]   \n                                                                &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln^2 x +C}\n$$\n\nAfter solving an integral using substitution, we should always double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{ln}^{2}⁡x+C) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({ln}^{2}⁡x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f20c3f56-ce97-548d-a8e0-c4a604acbc18" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8918 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "859100d6-9232-5a35-9f92-1ff67d69671a" :step/math-academy-id 118773 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101592}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101592 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100816 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100608 :question/id #uuid "8ea17e06-1248-510c-9e71-c57da0cb0d02" :question/math-academy-id "e-14405"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41b7d83f-3b9b-556c-9e49-13df9c3b597e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050628} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050690}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269163} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269173} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441899}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Functions Containing the Natural Logarithm"} :step/id #uuid "3b90fdb1-78e3-5033-ad6e-5d7b6eda947e" :step/math-academy-id 118774 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101593}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101593 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100817 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100609 :question/id #uuid "1579f932-6e7f-5daa-b001-54a9f6c236ad" :question/math-academy-id "e-14406"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa28f5c4-135e-576a-baa0-68c41b5139f6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374607} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384656}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Functions Containing Other Logarithms"} :step/id #uuid "bfc6f2ca-3c8f-5042-8e12-9a9d8d3e183b" :step/math-academy-id 118778 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101594}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101594 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186102030 :tutorial/content "Let's evaluate the following definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\int_{e}^{e^4} \\dfrac{3}{x \\sqrt{\\ln x}} \\, \\textrm{d}x }\n$$\n\nWe'll use the change of variable method, which comprises three steps:\n\n**Step 1:**  Change the variable.\n\nLet's introduce the new variable $u = \\ln x.$ Differentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{x}\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\textrm{d}u = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2:**  Find the new limits of integration.\n\nWe use the table below to change our limits of integration from $x$ to $u{:}$\n\n| $x$ | $e$ | $e^4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n\n**Step 3:** Evaluate the integral in the new variable $u.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{4}}\\frac{3}{x\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{4}}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{4}{u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6{u}^{1/2}\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =6(\\sqrt{4}-\\sqrt{1}) \\\\  & =6(2-1) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2add6e6b-5049-58e9-8095-b87e840d0844" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8931 :tutorial/title "Definite Integrals"} :step/id #uuid "fbecfd22-fc2c-53b8-959b-315e1af4f8fa" :step/math-academy-id 118782 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101595}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101595 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100818 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100610 :question/id #uuid "d2f49acf-ed91-510d-a0f1-41c50eeef9e7" :question/math-academy-id "e-14422"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37a7aff6-d1b2-5383-bb26-aa6363594b05" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048332}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101451} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101535} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297454} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297472} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297482} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297532} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319100} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319101} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342050} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342051} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342053} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374617} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375067} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441900}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Definite Integrals"} :step/id #uuid "8be2d12d-5050-599a-aca3-294a974cd35b" :step/math-academy-id 118783}] :activity/title "Integrating Logarithmic Functions Using Substitution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186101029}]}
